[Music]  can you draw a narrative threads through  your long and varied artistic career  that's actually a very good question  okay well I think something that I've  been interested in since I was a child  actually is trying to create situations  where much more happened than you  expected to happen so so you have a sort  of input and suddenly things proliferate  from that so for example one of the one  of my favorite games when I was a kid  was to try to make a little railway  engine take the longest possible route  to the bottom of a sort of spiral of  track I would create by resting the  rails on on books and adjusting  carefully and my idea was to get the  longest possible journey from top to  bottom that I could and I would adjust  adjust with playing cards and things  just to slow it down a little bit or  just so it got over that hump so that  that was an idea to sort of what's that  what's the most I can get out of this  simple action of putting a little  railway carriage on top of a spiral of  of downward spiral of rails then as time  went on that started develop into a sort  of an art practice I suppose where I  remember when I was 16 I was in hospital  and somebody brought me in some paints  and some card to paint on and I was  doing sort of abstract expressionist  paintings but but then going through  them through the pictures and cutting  out tiny little pieces like that so out  of one big splashy picture I would get  about a hundred tiny very exquisite  pictures which I sold to the doctors and  nurses actually the first work I ever  sold was in hospital with appendicitis  how much do they paid Oh lots of money  like two and sixpence so so then when I  started making music what interested me  really from the very beginning was  making kind of musical  machines of some kind musical systems  where for quite a small input there  would be a lot of music coming out I  basically want to make infinite music  machines so an example of one of those  that the boat the most simple example  you can imagine is the wind chime you  know a wind chime is a little machine  that you build has maybe five pipes and  the wind activates it and of course it  activates it differently all the time so  there's a there's a constantly changing  infinitely long piece of music from that  so I never made wind chimes but I was  inspired by the idea of making work in  that way so so essentially I think as I  want to make things that that surprised  me that I didn't visualize in advance  you know usually the idea of an artist  is that you have the whole thing in your  head and then somehow you physically  realize that you know so people imagine  that people like Beethoven had  symphonies in their head and then they  carefully wrote it all down and kind of  organized a way for an orchestra to  bring that into life that's never been  an interest for me in fact I don't  believe that it was for Beethoven either  actually I think that's a wrong  impression of how artists work that sort  of idea of the artist as an architect  belongs to architecture architects do  that a realize but I don't think  musicians generally do I think that we  are much more like gardeners with  planting seeds and then how they come up  is different every time you know and  constantly as surprising so I I moved  away from the idea of the artist as  architect meaning as somebody in  possession of a completely clear vision  of the final work I moved away from that  to the artist as gardener  somebody who plants a few seeds and then  watches them grow just as you the  audience watch them grow as one of the  most influential gardeners of the 20th  century do you take stock of your of the  people you've influenced I I don't  really think about it that much to be  honest I mean occasionally I hear  something and I think wow that's really  good at cents just like me  but on the other hand I sometimes hear  think's things and I think wow that's  really good it doesn't sound anything  like me at all so I'm kind of more  interested generally in the things that  aren't like what I do and what what  simple things haven't you yet taken and  made more complicated what's what  continues to motivate you well there  there are two levels of answer to that  question one is a very practical  day-to-day answer which is that there is  constantly new software being invented  which does things that nobody could ever  do before and so it's always been an  interest of mine to see what I could do  with it that nobody had thought of doing  for instance my early use of tape  recorders was really to do with the fact  that the tape recorder turned music from  something ephemeral something that was  in the air to something physical  something on tape so what can you do now  that sound is a physical entity that you  can edit and reverse and stretch and do  things to and so every every new  technology has the possibility of  rethinking the medium that it belongs to  of giving you a new a new way of using  that medium and of expanding the  envelope actually so that's a sort of a  day-to-day interest because there's so  much digital music technology coming out  all the time that if if I'm ever bored I  can just see what's new and try to keep  up with it but the longer term I think  the interest behind all of that is this  fascination I have with how things come  into being and  the classical view of how things come  into being is that very intelligent  things create other things so you know  classically God is at the top and then  he inspires people like Newton and  Darwin and Einstein and Shakespeare and  so on and through them  the brilliance of the deities sort of  spread out to the population and even  people who believe who don't believe in  God still kind of have that model in  their heads I think whereas I being a  Darwinian or a neo-darwinian think that  the most interesting thing about  evolution theory is the possibility that  complexity arises out of simplicity not  the other way around necessarily so so  I'm always interested in trying that  experiment in seeing how how something  incredibly beautiful and unexpected can  be engendered out of simple beginnings  and I think that that's a very very  important social message as well because  our social constructions are still based  on the idea of the pyramid with the  Godhead at the top and all the  intelligence at the top and the sort of  idiots at the bottom making use of what  comes out of it but I just don't think  that's how things come into being so  what don't see then I've been quite  daunted lately because I've been reading  a lot of recent climate science so that  does make you wonder whether any other  question is worth asking and are you  concerned with impact you know you're  clearly conscious of like social issues  do you think what you're doing how  you're applying your creativity now has  the best or most efficient impact  I mean impact is on me is what motivates  me I want to I want to find things out  you know I want to surprise myself or  things so so if if that isn't happening  I don't think I'm doing the right thing  on the other hand now I've become more  and more concerned with this issue of  how long have we got left on this planet  impact does become an issue you know I  suddenly think I suddenly for the first  time in my life want to change people's  minds I want to make people put their  attention somewhere that it may not be  at the moment so then impact does become  an issue and of course the question is  always do you do that best by just  carrying on with what you're good at or  do you say sorry I've got to put that to  one side and do this other thing and  many artists artists have had many  different answers to that on that  spectrum of possibilities you know Metis  spent the whole of the second world war  quietly painting in his studio hoping  that it would pass and then things would  go back to normal and his work would  have a place he was right as it happened  who but he might not be right if he  tried that this time people see yeah  people stopped listening once you  stopped being an artist and starts  trying to be something else yes there's  that that as well yes yeah where does  where does your credibility come from  and I think people have every right to  question your credibility if you  suddenly pop up in an area that you  don't apparently have any credentials  for I mean I think I do have some  credentials because I've been pursuing  this interest for for about 30 or 40  years but I haven't publicly been doing  that  the the question of environment and the  ecology in particular it's sort of the  in a way as the subject matter of my  work again how things come into being  how how systems work cybernetics was one  of the things I studied as a youngster  so so it's a line of thinking that I've  been doing for a for a long while but it  was sort of a private study until  recently for more debates talks and  interviews subscribe today to the  Institute of Arts and ideas at IAI TV  you

